{
  "id": "past",
  "title": "Talking about the past",
  "priority": 1,
  "order": 2,
  "explanation": "French has two everyday past tenses and you choose between them by what kind of past it is.\n\n**Imparfait** is for background and states: what was going on, how things were, what used to happen. Je voulais (I wanted), je pensais (I was thinking), je dormais, j'avais, je m'ennuyais, c'était, il y avait. If the English is \"was ...ing\", \"used to\" or a feeling or description, it is imparfait.\n\n**Passé composé** is for events: things that happened and finished. J'ai fait, j'ai pris, j'ai bu, j'ai lu, j'ai reçu, j'ai oublié, j'ai couru. It is built from avoir or être in the present plus the past participle, and the participle is the bit you keep getting wrong: *j'ai reçois* and *j'ai cours* are present-tense forms glued onto avoir. The participles are reçu and couru.\n\n**Être, not avoir**, for movement and change-of-state verbs (aller, venir, partir, arriver, revenir, rester, tomber, naître, mourir) and for all reflexive verbs. So nous sommes allés, je suis parti, je suis revenu, je me suis endormi. Never *nous avons allé*. With être the participle agrees with the subject: partie and revenue if a woman is speaking, allés for a group.\n\n**Devoir across tenses** (sheet):\n- je dois: I have to (now)\n- je devais: I had to / I was supposed to (ongoing, or never happened)\n- j'ai dû: I had to, and I did it\n- je devrai: I will have to\n\nSo *je devais le faire, mais je ne l'ai pas fait* (supposed to, didn't) against *j'ai dû partir tôt* (had to, and did).",
